1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,985
A test plan is a document that outlines

2
00:00:02,985 --> 00:00:06,495
all aspects that are required to complete testing.

3
00:00:06,495 --> 00:00:09,299
This generally includes the objective,

4
00:00:09,299 --> 00:00:11,985
scope and features to be tested.

5
00:00:11,985 --> 00:00:16,365
You'll work with the QA team to put this plan together.

6
00:00:16,365 --> 00:00:21,960
They'll also usually include additional information related to the testing strategy,

7
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:25,095
resourcing, timelines, and more.

8
00:00:25,095 --> 00:00:30,215
You'll collaborate most closely with them on defining the test cases.

9
00:00:30,215 --> 00:00:32,930
A test case describes a feature,

10
00:00:32,930 --> 00:00:34,925
how to use it or test it,

11
00:00:34,925 --> 00:00:37,040
and the expected behavior.

12
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:41,060
You may be asked to write the test cases or review them,

13
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:44,275
depending on how your QA team is set up.

14
00:00:44,275 --> 00:00:47,860
Test cases usually map to requirements,

15
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:52,670
which are generally broken into functional and non-functional requirements.

16
00:00:52,670 --> 00:00:58,160
A functional requirement defines a function of a system or its component where

17
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:04,310
a function is described as a specification of behavior between inputs and outputs.

18
00:01:04,310 --> 00:01:07,850
Another way to think about this is that

19
00:01:07,850 --> 00:01:13,490
a functional requirement describes what the user does and how the product should react.

20
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:17,960
Whereas a non-functional requirement specifies criteria that can be

21
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:22,985
used to judge the operation of a system rather than specific behaviors.

22
00:01:22,985 --> 00:01:27,185
Non-functional requirements describe how the system should work,

23
00:01:27,185 --> 00:01:29,030
not what it does.

24
00:01:29,030 --> 00:01:31,085
Let's walk through an example.

25
00:01:31,085 --> 00:01:33,425
Here's a functional requirement.

26
00:01:33,425 --> 00:01:38,415
After the user places an order a confirmation email is sent.

27
00:01:38,415 --> 00:01:43,670
In this case, the user is doing something which is placing an order,

28
00:01:43,670 --> 00:01:48,385
and the system reacts based on that by sending a confirmation email to the user.

29
00:01:48,385 --> 00:01:52,370
A related non-functional requirement would be that

30
00:01:52,370 --> 00:01:57,500
the confirmation email is sent within one minute of the order being placed.

31
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:02,720
In this case, the requirement is describing how the system must do something,

32
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:04,880
but not what it must do.

33
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:10,385
Functional and non-functional requirements are the basis for creating test cases.

34
00:02:10,385 --> 00:02:12,890
A test case includes a feature,

35
00:02:12,890 --> 00:02:15,545
the steps a user would take,

36
00:02:15,545 --> 00:02:19,565
the expected behavior, and our priority.

37
00:02:19,565 --> 00:02:23,455
Depending on the complexity of what's being tested,

38
00:02:23,455 --> 00:02:29,075
a test case may also include other prerequisites or test data.

39
00:02:29,075 --> 00:02:31,280
But basically at its core,

40
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:34,970
the test case outlines how to trigger the feature,

41
00:02:34,970 --> 00:02:37,085
how the feature should respond,

42
00:02:37,085 --> 00:02:41,045
and the priority of how important it is to test this case.

43
00:02:41,045 --> 00:02:45,275
The QA team is one of your key partners and they play

44
00:02:45,275 --> 00:02:50,135
an important role in making sure you build and ship high-quality products.

45
00:02:50,135 --> 00:02:54,790
Here are a couple of things to keep in mind when working with the QA team.

46
00:02:54,790 --> 00:02:57,590
Communication is key.

47
00:02:57,590 --> 00:03:03,080
Many times I've seen issues arise because of miscommunication and lack of clarity.

48
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:05,030
When working with QA,

49
00:03:05,030 --> 00:03:07,480
it's important to over-communicate.

50
00:03:07,480 --> 00:03:10,310
This helps to make sure that everyone is on

51
00:03:10,310 --> 00:03:14,930
the same page and has a shared understanding of expected behavior,

52
00:03:14,930 --> 00:03:18,980
priorities, timelines, or anything else.

53
00:03:18,980 --> 00:03:21,595
That leads me to the next thing.

54
00:03:21,595 --> 00:03:26,930
Timelines. You may think you know how long it will take to test something,

55
00:03:26,930 --> 00:03:30,395
but you likely don't have the full picture.

56
00:03:30,395 --> 00:03:36,640
Let QA drive the timelines for how long it'll take to fully test a feature or product.

57
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:40,625
Also, keep in mind that as you get closer to launch,

58
00:03:40,625 --> 00:03:45,395
there will likely be last-minute changes and pressure to meet deadlines.

59
00:03:45,395 --> 00:03:49,900
QA teams are often working in this stressful environment.

60
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:54,995
It's important to acknowledge this and identify how you can help to de-risk.

61
00:03:54,995 --> 00:03:59,965
You want to make things as easy as possible for your QA team.

62
00:03:59,965 --> 00:04:05,840
One major way that you can help to de-risk QA is through early involvement.

63
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:11,720
This means involving the QA team early on before the product is built.

64
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:19,595
A great time to reach out to QA is when the PRD is complete and handed off to the team.

65
00:04:19,595 --> 00:04:23,515
This gives QA the opportunity to review the feature,

66
00:04:23,515 --> 00:04:28,925
ask questions, and likely identify edge cases that you haven't thought of.

67
00:04:28,925 --> 00:04:35,125
You can then update your PRD to include the correct behavior for those edge cases.

68
00:04:35,125 --> 00:04:41,395
Quality assurance is a shared responsibility and doesn't belong to just the QA team.

69
00:04:41,395 --> 00:04:45,460
You should also think through how to test your product and

70
00:04:45,460 --> 00:04:49,885
involve the engineering team to help identify possible edge cases.

71
00:04:49,885 --> 00:04:55,560
Lastly, I promise you that you will run into issues and problems.

72
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:58,070
It's okay. It happens.

73
00:04:58,070 --> 00:05:02,020
The important thing is to not place blame on anyone.

74
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:05,270
Instead, focus on resolving issues and

75
00:05:05,270 --> 00:05:09,145
learning from the mistakes so that they're not repeated the next time.

76
00:05:09,145 --> 00:05:12,515
What happens when issues are found during QA?

77
00:05:12,515 --> 00:05:15,964
Well, once testing has been completed,

78
00:05:15,964 --> 00:05:21,470
you will get a report back of all the test cases and if they passed or failed.

79
00:05:21,470 --> 00:05:24,080
For any test cases that failed,

80
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:28,355
you'll want to make sure to create a new task to track each other.

81
00:05:28,355 --> 00:05:31,810
Often, the QA team will create these tasks for you.

82
00:05:31,810 --> 00:05:36,930
However, you will need to review and prioritize them.

